From: brian@indigo.nrccso.com (Brian Barnhart)
Date: Wed, 2 Mar 94 08:42:13 -0700
Subject: [#94-3-32] ENVIROBIZ: Costs of green business ...

Following the scavengers-as-environment-cleaners subthread:

The garage-sale /flea-market business has always struck me as interesting.
Particularly the folks who do this professionally.

So you have: 
1)Original purchase
2)Resale to garage-sale pro A
3)Resale to garage-sale pro B
4)Resale to consumer
5)-> as long as there is marginal value left, cycle continues

Actually, step 1) need not be a purchase. I've seen this happen with those
2'x3' carpet samples that the originator got for _free_. It makes me wonder
how many times some things are sold and the total amount of $ exchanged
through is lifetime, and finally how that relates to a commodity's value.

Actually, i guess its just a transfer of an item from one market to
another: from low-demand to high(er)-demand. Kind of like locks&dams
to go up rivers. One example would be baseball-card business: depending
on where you are the card's _value_ can vary tremendously, and there's
$ to be made in the differential. Through lots of secondary-market
transactions.

Ultimately, the commodities still end up as refuse but the 'useful life'
can be many times the primary-market's intended replacement interval.

just a thought to share.

brian



